{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1219 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "63339f3cdda01df01bbd398b498c6c0050ad76eef4ada39276af0feedd9d28e3" :edb/db-before-t 1218 :edb/db-after-t 1219 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534531 50 #inst "2026-10-07T20:41:59.208Z" 13194139534531 true] [17592186096560 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293410 13194139534531 true] [17592186096560 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293417 13194139534531 true] [17592186096560 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293424 13194139534531 true] [17592186096671 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293427 13194139534531 true] [17592186096671 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293430 13194139534531 true] [17592186293410 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293411 13194139534531 true] [17592186293410 1089 "q-11311" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293410 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534531 true] [17592186293410 1100 "Find the area of the closed region between the curve $x=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y$ and the $y$-axis." 13194139534531 true] [17592186293410 1113 #uuid "9fb1998f-90f1-521a-ae9b-ace4a950305a" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293410 1145 "First, we find $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ y(\\frac{1}{2}y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/assets/29cf73562152cb8201c8e93545ca7f8416aa8412217cfc9dcdaf87e5b794d971.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=2.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(2)^{3}}{3}-0)-(\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293411 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293412 13194139534531 true] [17592186293411 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293413 13194139534531 true] [17592186293411 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293414 13194139534531 true] [17592186293411 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293415 13194139534531 true] [17592186293411 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293416 13194139534531 true] [17592186293411 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534531 true] [17592186293411 1041 "selection" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293411 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293413 13194139534531 true] [17592186293411 1110 #uuid "e4d7772d-3749-572b-813a-7025097ac227" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293412 1034 "\\frac{13}{3}" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293412 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534531 true] [17592186293412 1111 #uuid "0e63ad45-de5b-5a00-9e2c-e1b858ca6b4b" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293413 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293413 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534531 true] [17592186293413 1111 #uuid "7a685c74-4a61-578a-b626-989e8ca5267d" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293414 1034 "\\frac{8}{3}" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293414 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534531 true] [17592186293414 1111 #uuid "45afdfcf-7637-5278-940a-1f6d676078da" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293415 1034 "\\frac{5}{3}" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293415 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534531 true] [17592186293415 1111 #uuid "0c2ffd00-ee9c-5c75-8134-8169c7a5f74d" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293416 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293416 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534531 true] [17592186293416 1111 #uuid "5d43e8bf-f783-5fb1-8891-2405e6a25f50" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293417 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293418 13194139534531 true] [17592186293417 1089 "q-117482" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293417 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534531 true] [17592186293417 1100 "Find the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=16-{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis." 13194139534531 true] [17592186293417 1113 #uuid "f33ac467-8172-5f8f-a360-999df7af12d7" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293417 1145 "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{4} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/assets/488788313bd32999611a520ec26e4ba6fd68a56e0333a7252a0faaf7107cad90.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=16-{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=-2$ to $y=2.$ \nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(16-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =16\\cdot y{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\cdot (2-(-2))-(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{(-2)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =64-\\frac{64}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293418 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293419 13194139534531 true] [17592186293418 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293420 13194139534531 true] [17592186293418 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293421 13194139534531 true] [17592186293418 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293422 13194139534531 true] [17592186293418 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293423 13194139534531 true] [17592186293418 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534531 true] [17592186293418 1041 "selection" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293418 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293419 13194139534531 true] [17592186293418 1110 #uuid "04c67ffb-9bb6-5b55-98ee-0be43b8f764c" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293419 1034 "\\frac{256}{5}" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293419 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534531 true] [17592186293419 1111 #uuid "59adfa04-5ac1-5141-bb4a-6c8a421eb8a5" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293420 1034 "\\frac{64}{5}" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293420 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534531 true] [17592186293420 1111 #uuid "8c6aa69a-8d3c-5192-8f08-b03a6342c3c0" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293421 1034 "\\frac{128}{5}" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293421 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534531 true] [17592186293421 1111 #uuid "c449a0ff-aef1-5b7e-ae65-87c9eb22687a" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293422 1034 "\\frac{512}{5}" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293422 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534531 true] [17592186293422 1111 #uuid "f97482ae-b0a8-58c0-951a-8f76c59c83a1" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293423 1034 "51" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293423 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534531 true] [17592186293423 1111 #uuid "e6bfe423-acc4-5c1a-a140-d5373dfae41a" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293424 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293425 13194139534531 true] [17592186293424 1089 "q-346674" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293424 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534531 true] [17592186293424 1100 "Find the area $A$ of the closed region between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y$ and the $y$-axis for $y\\le 0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293424 1113 #uuid "b02a57a4-6817-5d59-84b9-fa9676c32001" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293424 1145 "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}y({y}^{4}-16) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}y({y}^{2}-4)({y}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}y(y-2)(y+2)({y}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/assets/03737836a352eafaf486e7e5f71d882ac39715e788a0c9113eb809b185584bdc.png)\n\nWe want the area between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-2$ to $y=0.$\nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y-3{\\int }_{-2}^{0}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{y}^{6}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}(0-(-64))-\\frac{3}{2}(0-4) \\\\  & =-2+6 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293425 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293426 13194139534531 true] [17592186293425 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534531 true] [17592186293425 1041 "field-1" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293425 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293426 13194139534531 true] [17592186293425 1110 #uuid "724754c0-f45b-57b2-9042-56fefca97246" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293426 1034 "4" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293426 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534531 true] [17592186293426 1111 #uuid "0f742ada-acb5-5e20-9b9d-e2ba3383764f" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293427 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293428 13194139534531 true] [17592186293427 1089 "q-346735" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293427 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534531 true] [17592186293427 1100 "Find the area $A$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x+\\frac{1}{4}}$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=-2$ and $y=-1.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293427 1113 #uuid "2ee1add0-181d-5882-bad5-e546b4df954f" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293427 1145 "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{x+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =x+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ x & ={y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}\\,|}_{-2}^{-1}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-4)-\\frac{1}{4}[-1-(-2)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So, the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-4|=4.\n$$" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293428 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293429 13194139534531 true] [17592186293428 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534531 true] [17592186293428 1041 "field-1" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293428 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293429 13194139534531 true] [17592186293428 1110 #uuid "963feaf7-7851-5318-933b-840507a0361e" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293429 1034 "4" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293429 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534531 true] [17592186293429 1111 #uuid "829a8608-e619-593c-b76f-adc9a7090aba" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293430 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293431 13194139534531 true] [17592186293430 1089 "q-78887" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293430 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534531 true] [17592186293430 1100 "Find the area between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x+1}{4}},$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=-1$ and $y=0.$" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293430 1113 #uuid "f6ae8b55-a31f-51be-b137-158d1f0dcaf6" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293430 1145 "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x+1}{4}} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =\\frac{x+1}{4} \\\\ 4{y}^{3} & =x+1 \\\\ x & =4{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4{y}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{0}-y{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =(0-1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is $|-2|=2.$" 13194139534531 true] [17592186293431 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293432 13194139534531 true] [175